WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.600
I'm an infectious disease epidemiologist

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:07.440
at Kent State university in Ohio.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.639
I mostly work on what are called

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.800
zoonotic diseases

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:12.400
so those are diseases that are

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.440
transmitted between animals

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.600
and people and earlier this week I wrote

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.920
a column for Quanta Magazine about

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.880
how our existing knowledge of

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.920
coronaviruses can

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.599
potentially help us inform, knowing

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.519
and understanding what's going on with

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:29.840
the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:30.560
And so I'm here today on the Quanta

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.960
Magazine

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.399
YouTube channel to answer some

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.520
additional questions that have come

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:38.559
in on social media, in the comments of

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.600
that article

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:42.960
and elsewhere and there is a

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.480
function where you can also ask

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.920
questions during the talk and those will

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.000
be relayed to me.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.760
So I guess I'll answer as many of those

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.760
as possible.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.000
And I will get started with a few that

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.840
already came in

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
from various locations already. So one

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.239
question:

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.039
Does immunity conferred from

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:04.239
infection

00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:06.159
by a cold coronavirus -- so there are four

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:07.840
other types of coronaviruses that cause

00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:10.240
kind of the common cold

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.680
in humans that have been identified

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.799
previously --

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.640
so does immunity to these provide

00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:18.479
prophylaxis or I guess immunity to

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.880
COVID-19?

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:22.640
And is anyone asking asymptomatic or

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.000
immune cases about recent cold

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.320
Infections?

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.119
And the answer is, I mean we're not

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.040
100 percent

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:30.560
sure but probably not. There doesn't seem

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.280
to be a lot of

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.159
cross-protective immunity between

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.680
the various

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:40.000
coronavirus species and even

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.720
when immunity develops it seems to wane

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.399
relatively

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:47.040
quickly. So that's looking at both

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:50.320
patients who had had SARS, kind of the

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.960
original SARS from 2002 and 2003

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.079
and following those over time, at least

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.719
the antibody response

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:57.280
seemed to drop off pretty dramatically

00:01:56.719 --> 00:02:00.000
over

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.600
a period of about one to three years.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.119
Looking at some of the other human

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.240
coronaviruses, the common cold

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:05.840
coronavirus,

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.239
that immunity also seems to wane

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.680
relatively quickly, so

00:02:08.239 --> 00:02:13.200
you would have to have had a cold

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.840
relatively recently which is one of the

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.640
things that they asked, are people asking

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.760
if they've had colds? And I don't know

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.520
any studies that are

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.200
doing that currently but you would have

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.599
to do more than just ask about colds.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.720
What you would really want to do

00:02:25.599 --> 00:02:31.280
is to set up a study to sample

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.360
people over time and not only ask about

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:37.200
colds because those can be caused --

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.560
the common cold

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.480
only about 20 percent of those are caused by

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.959
coronaviruses so the other

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.480
80 percent or so are other types of viruses that

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:47.680
would not be protective.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.720
So what you want to do is really

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.959
sample over time

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.720
and look at people who are getting those

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:55.360
common cold viruses

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.480
and then look at if they are able to be

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.120
infected with

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.440
SARS coronavirus 2. A problem with doing

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.959
that right now of course is that

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:08.239
our testing supplies are very limited.

00:03:04.959 --> 00:03:09.840
Even things like you know PPE,

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:11.920
personal protective equipment for people

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.519
who are doing the sampling, the swabs

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.879
that we use to do the sampling

00:03:13.519 --> 00:03:17.680
are also in short supply, so that type of

00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:19.760
study

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.840
would be difficult to do right now to

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.440
get any kind of definitive answers but

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.120
I guarantee people are probably looking

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.920
at those types of things in other animal

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.159
models

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.599
and other aspects as well. So that is

00:03:28.159 --> 00:03:30.879
definitely something that's on people's

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:32.159
minds.

00:03:30.879 --> 00:03:34.239
And one thing I wanted to add with that

00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:36.319
was that's also kind of one

00:03:34.239 --> 00:03:38.959
hypothesis that's been put forward

00:03:36.319 --> 00:03:40.640
regarding why kids may not be as

00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:42.959
seriously infected with this,

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.680
may not have symptoms that are as

00:03:42.959 --> 00:03:46.879
serious

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:50.159
as adults get. You know kids

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:52.959
are germy and may have

00:03:50.159 --> 00:03:54.400
more colds from coronaviruses more

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:56.159
recently than adults have,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.760
so that's another thing that's being

00:03:56.159 --> 00:04:00.080
investigated,

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.959
although I don't know, you know, I know

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.400
it's been

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:03.840
bounced around the research

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.080
community, so I know it's on people's

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:10.319
minds.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.319
All right some other ones, let's see.

00:04:11.439 --> 00:04:15.200
All right. In the realm of

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.479
neurological changes

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.400
and the search for characteristic

00:04:16.479 --> 00:04:19.120
symptoms which might be easier to

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.600
detect

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.199
than loss of taste and smell

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.160
has anyone thought to look for changes

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:29.440
in hearing,

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:31.040
maybe loss of upper octaves?

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:32.720
I don't know about that. That's

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.000
really interesting but

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:36.080
I don't know that that would necessarily

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.759
be easier to detect,

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:39.600
because you do have to have equipment

00:04:37.759 --> 00:04:43.280
to do that, and it also helps to have

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:45.840
like a before and after to know

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.360
if before coronavirus infection people

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.120
were able to detect those

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.880
upper octaves anyway, because of course

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.720
that's something that

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.560
many adults lose over time just from

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.160
being around loud noises and going to

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:01.360
concerts and things like that.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.199
I have not seen any evidence that the

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:06.800
virus

00:05:03.199 --> 00:05:09.199
affects nerves of the auditory system.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.840
With the olfactory system we do know

00:05:09.199 --> 00:05:14.639
that

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.120
this virus and other coronavirus as well

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:18.400
can

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:20.080
enter through olfactory nerves and

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.320
can affect some of the tissue around

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.160
those and may

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.000
cause kind of pathology in that

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.400
manner and that may be what

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.240
causes loss of sense and tastes but

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.080
we're not even sure of that yet.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.840
So I think there's a whole lot of

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:37.199
open questions around

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.199
those neurological issues with

00:05:37.199 --> 00:05:40.800
with SARS coronavirus too

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:42.560
that are still really big unknowns and I

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:46.320
haven't seen anything regarding

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.320
hearing for that.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:54.000
All right. Twitter. So

00:05:51.199 --> 00:05:56.080
I have a question, if the coronavirus

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.600
family has a low rate of mutations

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:00.319
why has it been so difficult to make a

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.160
vaccine? And this is from Becca O.

00:06:00.319 --> 00:06:03.919
The motifs and structures in them are

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.479
relatively conserved and why are they

00:06:03.919 --> 00:06:08.000
not

00:06:04.479 --> 00:06:10.000
very immunogenic? And so for

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.440
vaccines there are a whole lot of

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.479
things that really

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:17.919
need to come together to create

00:06:14.479 --> 00:06:19.360
an effective and safe vaccine. And so

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:20.880
one of them is the mutation rate,

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.120
obviously if something mutates really

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.280
quickly like influenza,

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:26.800
it makes it more difficult to create a

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:28.639
vaccine because

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.160
it mutates around that, and so you

00:06:28.639 --> 00:06:32.319
have to update it frequently.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:34.319
But that's not the only issue. So you

00:06:32.319 --> 00:06:37.360
have to find you know good antigens,

00:06:34.319 --> 00:06:39.280
ones that will generate

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:40.880
a robust immune response and ones that

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.720
will not you know harm the host.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:44.160
That’s been you know the challenge

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.120
with this.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.440
So we do have a good target antigen

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.599
in

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.960
the spike protein. So this is the one

00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:54.319
that is used

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:56.319
by the virus to attach to host cells.

00:06:54.319 --> 00:06:57.599
And so there was a lot of work on this

00:06:56.319 --> 00:07:02.080
in the SARS

00:06:57.599 --> 00:07:05.039
era. So when that virus was recognized

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.400
and its kind of pathogenic properties

00:07:05.039 --> 00:07:08.560
were realized and

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.840
and we saw how harmful this could be

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:12.880
there was a big infusion

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:15.039
in funding for vaccines for

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:16.560
for SARS coronavirus. And so there are a

00:07:15.039 --> 00:07:18.479
lot of groups working on it and a lot of

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.080
them had some candidate vaccines that

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:24.080
ended up getting tested,

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:26.720
some in animal models. But then

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.240
you know SARS went away and we were

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:30.720
able to contain it.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:33.039
And with the other serious human

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:35.759
coronavirus, MERS coronavirus,

00:07:33.039 --> 00:07:36.960
it really hasn't created the kinds of

00:07:35.759 --> 00:07:39.680
epidemics

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.880
that that SARS did, so there was just

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:45.199
kind of a loss

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:48.479
of interest in coronavirus vaccines

00:07:45.199 --> 00:07:51.759
for you know maybe like 2008

00:07:48.479 --> 00:07:52.319
till basically the present. So a lot

00:07:51.759 --> 00:07:53.759
of

00:07:52.319 --> 00:07:55.520
things got developed and then never

00:07:53.759 --> 00:07:57.919
followed up on. So that's one big problem

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:00.960
is that they just never got

00:07:57.919 --> 00:08:03.599
into you know robust human tests for

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:05.520
safety and efficacy. We were just never

00:08:03.599 --> 00:08:08.800
able to do those with the previous

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:10.840
candidates. So they're there but one

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:14.240
of the other problems potentially was

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:16.800
that when they used the whole

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:17.360
protein so the entire protein they did

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.400
find

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:22.319
that in some cases it seemed to cause

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:23.759
more harm to some of the vaccinated

00:08:22.319 --> 00:08:25.680
animals than protection.

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:28.080
And so this was through a mechanism

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.360
called antibody dependent enhancement.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:32.640
We see this with some other viral

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:35.200
diseases, most notably dengue virus

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:37.200
where if you're infected once and then

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.399
infected again with a different strain

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.159
of dengue

00:08:38.399 --> 00:08:43.919
you're actually more likely to have

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:45.600
severe outcomes, to get dengue

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:48.000
hemorrhagic fever

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:49.040
than those who had only been

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.200
infected once.

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:52.640
So those antibodies don't necessarily

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:54.959
protect you they can actually

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:56.800
enhance the ability of the virus to

00:08:54.959 --> 00:08:58.800
potentially enter host cells and

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:00.240
and a number of other things that

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:02.160
lead to a more severe

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:03.360
outcome the second time you're infected.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.600
So they saw that with some of these

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:07.120
candidate vaccines and so

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:08.720
instead of using the whole spike

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:11.839
protein they have kind of

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:15.120
chopped it up to look at the

00:09:11.839 --> 00:09:19.200
smallest piece that is both

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:21.760
you know effective as an antigen

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.360
but doesn't cause this kind of antibody

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:26.480
dependent enhancement. So

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:30.240
that's what they're doing for most of

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:31.839
the candidate vaccines, is some

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.000
pieces or parts of the spike

00:09:31.839 --> 00:09:36.000
protein. And I would will say that there

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:37.760
are about a hundred different

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.120
vaccine candidates that are in some form

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:40.480
of development right now so there's a

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:43.600
lot of them out there.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:46.240
So it's really been just that we

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:48.560
kind of lost a lot of that work

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:50.000
in the last decade or so and now

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:53.040
we're trying to play catch-up

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:55.360
in you know a period of three months or so.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:56.720
So it's not that necessarily we

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:59.040
don't know that we,

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:00.480
you know that's super difficult to make

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:02.240
or that we can't make one, or that we

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:04.079
really failed in the past it's just that

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:06.000
we have stopped in the past because

00:10:04.079 --> 00:10:10.720
we didn't have much reason to

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:12.240
to go on. So I think there's a lot of

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:14.320
hope that we will be able to create a

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.920
vaccine for this but

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:17.440
it's just kind of overcoming some

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:19.440
of those challenges

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:21.760
where we left off previously. And now you

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:24.640
have to pick up those research programs

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:24.640
almost from scratch.

00:10:25.519 --> 00:10:28.800
All right.

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:36.320
So let's see, so this is from YouTube.

00:10:32.959 --> 00:10:39.360
So can't social distancing only work

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.480
if we keep that going until we get a

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.680
vaccine?

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:43.360
Assuming we stopped social

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:44.800
distancing before a vaccine, aren’t we just

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:46.959
going to get a second wave

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.480
until we hit herd immunity, and therefore

00:10:46.959 --> 00:10:49.760
social distancing didn't actually do

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:51.920
anything?

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:53.680
So that's one of the things that we're

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:58.399
working out right now.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:00.079
So what we've done so far, there is

00:10:58.399 --> 00:11:01.279
evidence that social distancing in the

00:11:00.079 --> 00:11:04.880
United States

00:11:01.279 --> 00:11:07.040
and elsewhere has worked pretty well,

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:07.920
that we have slowed down the spread of

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:10.480
This.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:11.279
It's estimated that across the United

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:14.800
States

00:11:11.279 --> 00:11:17.760
the basic reproductive number right now

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:18.959
averaging again everything is

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:22.560
right about at one.

00:11:18.959 --> 00:11:24.399
So that means that on average

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:26.480
each person who is infected spreads it

00:11:24.399 --> 00:11:27.120
to one other person so this is better

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:28.959
than

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.800
what it was initially in the outbreak,

00:11:28.959 --> 00:11:33.839
which is estimated to be

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:35.760
around three. And this is because

00:11:33.839 --> 00:11:37.760
most people are doing this

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:41.040
social distancing, most

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:42.399
areas have implemented that. But

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:44.399
a couple things. That means one it's

00:11:42.399 --> 00:11:45.519
still spreading even if the

00:11:44.399 --> 00:11:48.399
reproductive

00:11:45.519 --> 00:11:49.680
number is lower it still is not

00:11:48.399 --> 00:11:51.120
extinguished, because

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:52.399
on average still each person is

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.720
spreading it to at least one other

00:11:52.399 --> 00:11:56.480
person which means it will continue to

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:58.000
to spread even if it's slower than it

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:01.839
would have spread

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:04.720
you know in March. And

00:12:01.839 --> 00:12:05.440
sorry my dogs are running outside and

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:08.480
distracting me.

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:10.000
And so it means it is still

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:13.440
spreading and then of course some states

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:15.760
are reopening so Georgia, Texas others

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:17.440
are loosening some of their restrictions

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:18.399
on some of these social gatherings and

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:20.320
opening up

00:12:18.399 --> 00:12:21.680
you know hair salons and tattoo

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:24.399
parlors and

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:24.959
restaurants in some cases. So you do have

00:12:24.399 --> 00:12:28.160
that

00:12:24.959 --> 00:12:29.360
potential for additional cases from that

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:32.639
right.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:34.959
So as

00:12:32.639 --> 00:12:36.320
public health experts what we would

00:12:34.959 --> 00:12:38.800
really like

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:40.000
is to move from the mitigation

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:40.959
standpoint which is basically where

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:44.560
we're just trying

00:12:40.959 --> 00:12:46.720
to keep this at a level where it doesn't

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:50.000
overwhelm our hospital systems

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:52.560
where you know we can keep people

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.839
alive if they are seriously ill where we

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.360
have enough medical professionals to

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:56.720
deal with this and enough hospital beds

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:59.440
and things like that which we've

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:01.519
pretty well pretty much done so far,

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:03.600
to going back to a containment strategy

00:13:01.519 --> 00:13:07.200
and so for that

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:09.279
you need to be able to test people.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:10.480
Right now there are very few areas that

00:13:09.279 --> 00:13:13.760
can test people

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:16.240
with more mild infections.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:18.480
Most of the tests are still done on

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:20.000
people with more serious infections

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:22.079
and also on healthcare workers who need

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:25.120
to know if they are

00:13:22.079 --> 00:13:26.959
are positive for COVID-19

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:28.320
and they would have to take time off

00:13:26.959 --> 00:13:31.519
off work

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:33.040
and not be around patients. So that's

00:13:31.519 --> 00:13:33.680
where most of the tests are going right

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.200
now.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:38.240
What we really need is more tests so we

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:42.320
can test anyone who has symptoms

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:45.120
and isolate individuals who are

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:46.079
positive and then find out who they have

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.320
been exposed to

00:13:46.079 --> 00:13:49.600
over the last you know five or seven

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:52.639
days

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:54.240
and put them into quarantine so that

00:13:52.639 --> 00:13:56.800
you can basically stop the spread of

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:59.199
this virus by getting infected people

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:00.880
out of society for a brief time until

00:13:59.199 --> 00:14:03.360
they're no longer infectious

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:05.120
and keeping those who have been exposed

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:06.480
from spreading the virus before they

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:07.680
become symptomatic

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:10.320
before they even realize they're

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.600
Infected. So that's what like South Korea

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:12.000
has done, basically that's been their

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:15.519
model,

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:17.440
is this test trace isolate.

00:14:15.519 --> 00:14:18.959
But right now, we just we don't have the

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:20.320
ability to do that, we don't have the

00:14:18.959 --> 00:14:20.720
tests we don't have as I mentioned

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:22.720
before

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:24.560
personal protective equipment for people

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:25.600
who are doing the tests to keep them

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:27.600
safe while they're

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:30.399
taking samples. We don't have the

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:31.440
ability to turn this around rapidly.

00:14:30.399 --> 00:14:34.320
You know one of the things that we

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:36.880
really need is to be able to take a test

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:38.160
and get people their results really

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:41.600
quickly which we don't

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:44.320
have right now and then

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:46.160
having this kind of mass quarantine, I

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:47.680
mean not of the whole population but of

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:50.800
individuals who have been exposed

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:52.639
also is logistically difficult.

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:53.920
So that needs to have a really good

00:14:52.639 --> 00:14:57.120
protocol in place

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:59.120
and we don't have any of that so

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:02.079
that's still what we're hoping for.

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:02.959
You know no one wants to

00:15:02.079 --> 00:15:04.959
stay in

00:15:02.959 --> 00:15:06.399
in kind of a stay at home a lockdown

00:15:04.959 --> 00:15:08.320
situation for the next

00:15:06.399 --> 00:15:11.519
you know two years or whatever until

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:13.839
potentially we have an effective vaccine.

00:15:11.519 --> 00:15:15.120
But you know what you're going to be

00:15:13.839 --> 00:15:17.519
doing is if you

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:18.880
relax those social distancing measures

00:15:17.519 --> 00:15:19.600
then yes, you're probably going to see

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:21.519
another

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:23.680
increase in cases and then you probably

00:15:21.519 --> 00:15:24.480
have to restrict those measures again

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:27.440
and

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:29.120
do this over and over which um I don't

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:31.040
think is good for

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:32.320
you know for society for mental health

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:36.000
for any of this,

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:37.199
so so I’m hopeful that still at some

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:40.240
point we can

00:15:37.199 --> 00:15:41.759
ramp up that that testing and do that

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:43.279
instead of being in just this kind of

00:15:41.759 --> 00:15:46.079
full society

00:15:43.279 --> 00:15:48.000
mitigation effects until hopefully we

00:15:46.079 --> 00:15:51.519
we do get a vaccine

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:54.880
at some point. All right.

00:15:51.519 --> 00:15:57.120
So, let's see for our next questions here…

00:15:59.839 --> 00:16:03.860
All right

00:16:04.079 --> 00:16:08.720
So, if we do get a vaccine and if

00:16:07.279 --> 00:16:09.759
reinfection after a year is possible -- this

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:12.720
is from the Quanta

00:16:09.759 --> 00:16:14.560
comment section -- then even the best

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:16.639
vaccine will have to be given every year.

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:19.040
So that's a question from Ted.

00:16:16.639 --> 00:16:20.320
And we don't know. Again, it depends on

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:23.600
what ends up

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:25.440
being the vaccine that kind of makes

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:26.720
it to the finish line first or

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:28.959
the one that ends up being most

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:30.880
effective

00:16:28.959 --> 00:16:32.639
and with 100 candidates out there

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:34.320
it's really hard to say what that is

00:16:32.639 --> 00:16:36.959
going to be right now.

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:38.639
So most vaccines you know are based on

00:16:36.959 --> 00:16:42.000
the model of immunity that

00:16:38.639 --> 00:16:44.560
the host would have naturally

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:45.199
but there are ways to kind of tweak that.

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:48.800
So

00:16:45.199 --> 00:16:51.839
you can in some cases conjugate

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:52.560
additional proteins to the main

00:16:51.839 --> 00:16:55.440
antigen

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:56.639
of the virus and make the immune

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:58.800
response stronger

00:16:56.639 --> 00:16:59.920
for example. We do this with some of

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:02.800
the bacterial

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:04.480
vaccines that makes them

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:06.319
last longer

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:08.079
and makes them a little bit more robust

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:09.520
so there may be potential for that.

00:17:08.079 --> 00:17:11.600
There may be potential to put this

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:14.559
into kind of live vectors,

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:15.600
not the live virus, it's not the live

00:17:14.559 --> 00:17:18.720
coronavirus but

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:19.199
other viruses that can replicate in the

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:21.679
body

00:17:19.199 --> 00:17:23.199
and kind of introduce the body to maybe

00:17:21.679 --> 00:17:24.799
pieces and parts of that spike protein

00:17:23.199 --> 00:17:28.000
as I mentioned before.

00:17:24.799 --> 00:17:29.360
That may be more antigenic so we

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:30.799
just don't really know it

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:32.559
may be something that would have to be

00:17:30.799 --> 00:17:35.440
boosted every year,

00:17:32.559 --> 00:17:36.480
we don't know at this point. But it

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:38.960
may also be that

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:39.600
you know even if immunity does wane

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:43.840
after

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:46.720
a year or who knows it may be that

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:48.160
future infections are still more mild

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:50.400
than the original one

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:52.080
so even if you don't have kind of

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:54.400
complete you know protective

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:55.600
antibody titers it might be enough to

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:57.440
keep you from getting a serious

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:59.120
infection and then

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:00.080
that might be all we care about. You

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:01.440
know you might not have to get

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:02.960
revaccinated so

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:05.600
there are a ton of open questions about

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:08.240
that right now and honestly there's

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:08.799
more questions than answers so

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:10.080
yeah so,

00:18:08.799 --> 00:18:12.080
unfortunately, I just have to say we just

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:16.080
really don't

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:18.480
know right now. All right.

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:18.480
Let's see.

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:28.000
From YouTube live from Molly Given.

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:29.520
How likely is it that we will discover a

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:31.280
really powerful drug that cures

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:33.760
COVID-19 quickly before a vaccine

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:36.000
gets licensed for widespread use?

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:37.840
That’s a good question. I just did

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:40.000
an article

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:42.400
for another outlet looking at some of

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:45.520
the drugs that are being used

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:46.400
and for most of those drugs, not all

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:49.760
but most

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:51.919
that are being examined for treatment

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:52.799
for COVID-19, a lot of them are

00:18:51.919 --> 00:18:55.840
already in use

00:18:52.799 --> 00:18:59.919
for other infections or other

00:18:55.840 --> 00:19:00.320
areas. So if we could find something

00:18:59.919 --> 00:19:02.960
that

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:03.919
is effective and it has already been

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:05.520
licensed for

00:19:03.919 --> 00:19:07.039
something else, it's already known to be

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:09.120
you know relatively safe

00:19:07.039 --> 00:19:11.360
and has already gone through all the

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:12.799
approvals it's much easier to take those

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:16.559
and kind of repurpose them

00:19:12.799 --> 00:19:18.240
for treatment for COVID-19 than it is

00:19:16.559 --> 00:19:19.280
to develop something completely de novo

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:21.520
where you have to

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:23.440
you know determine its safety profile

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:25.919
and everything else.

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:28.160
So if we find one of those that are

00:19:25.919 --> 00:19:30.720
already approved for other

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:31.440
infections or other conditions then yes

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:34.480
I think

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:36.640
that those could be in place before

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:38.640
we get to a vaccine.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:40.559
But from you know the tests we've seen

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:41.679
so far and of course there are many that

00:19:40.559 --> 00:19:43.200
are ongoing so

00:19:41.679 --> 00:19:45.600
who knows what the outcome of those

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:48.640
will be too,

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:51.039
I haven't been particularly impressed

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:52.640
with the results that we've seen so

00:19:51.039 --> 00:19:55.280
far. We saw

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:56.400
the results from some of the Remdesivir

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:58.480
trials the other day,

00:19:56.400 --> 00:19:59.600
and the ones in China really didn't

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:01.280
go anywhere.

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:02.640
The NIH ones that were done here in the

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:04.559
United States seem to

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:06.159
maybe show some progress but that's also

00:20:04.559 --> 00:20:07.600
an IV drug which makes it just a lot

00:20:06.159 --> 00:20:10.320
more difficult

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:11.760
to get to a larger number of people,

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:14.000
especially because it seems that one

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:17.440
really might be better

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:19.840
if used early but you know if you have a

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:22.880
person with

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:24.640
mild symptoms

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:26.080
are you going to give them an IV drug

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:27.760
and how is that going to be used? So

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:29.600
I think there are a lot of a lot

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:29.840
of issues that need to be worked

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:31.760
out

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:32.880
and of course, a lot of other drugs are

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:36.640
under investigation,

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:38.000
so it kind of depends on if those

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:40.480
are already approved

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:42.640
and could just be kind of co-opted for

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:44.240
COVID-19 or if they're completely new

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:48.000
and need to go through those studies

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:51.440
which would take longer.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:51.440
All right.

