00:44:35.599 --> 00:44:39.440
immune in order to reach that herd

00:44:38.319 --> 00:44:41.119
immunity threshold.

00:44:39.440 --> 00:44:42.640
Okay now remember herd immunity is not

00:44:41.119 --> 00:44:44.240
like a switch, it's not all or nothing,

00:44:42.640 --> 00:44:45.680
the higher number of people

00:44:44.240 --> 00:44:46.800
that you have in the population that are

00:44:45.680 --> 00:44:49.280
immune

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:50.000
the more difficult it makes for that

00:44:49.280 --> 00:44:52.560
virus to

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:53.200
find a susceptible host and to spread so

00:44:52.560 --> 00:44:54.800
you know

00:44:53.200 --> 00:44:57.280
any immunity in the population is better

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:59.599
than none even if it's not kind of at

00:44:57.280 --> 00:45:01.200
that herd immunity threshold yet.

00:44:59.599 --> 00:45:02.720
But for this again because we have so

00:45:01.200 --> 00:45:03.359
many open questions about immunity we

00:45:02.720 --> 00:45:06.560
just don't know.

00:45:03.359 --> 00:45:08.880
So if we would

00:45:06.560 --> 00:45:10.000
go over the course of say you know 12

00:45:08.880 --> 00:45:12.800
months all right

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:13.920
and 40 percent of the population would have been

00:45:12.800 --> 00:45:17.280
infected

00:45:13.920 --> 00:45:19.599
with SARS coronavirus 2

00:45:17.280 --> 00:45:21.680
in theory 40 of the population then

00:45:19.599 --> 00:45:24.000
would be immune.

00:45:21.680 --> 00:45:25.280
But what if that immunity wanes after a

00:45:24.000 --> 00:45:27.599
year so you have the people who were

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:30.079
infected at the very beginning

00:45:27.599 --> 00:45:30.880
have maybe you know are maybe no longer

00:45:30.079 --> 00:45:33.760
functionally

00:45:30.880 --> 00:45:35.119
immune so you're not really at 40 percent of the

00:45:33.760 --> 00:45:37.520
population anymore,

00:45:35.119 --> 00:45:39.040
you're at a lower level than that. So

00:45:37.520 --> 00:45:41.119
those are some of the questions that we

00:45:39.040 --> 00:45:42.560
just don't have answers to and we

00:45:41.119 --> 00:45:43.599
won't have answers. We just can't have

00:45:42.560 --> 00:45:45.839
answers

00:45:43.599 --> 00:45:47.520
without following people over time so

00:45:45.839 --> 00:45:48.000
it's only something that we will know

00:45:47.520 --> 00:45:50.560
after

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:52.800
you know one year or three years how

00:45:50.560 --> 00:45:55.359
long that immunity

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:57.040
lasts and if people are really protected

00:45:55.359 --> 00:45:58.720
from reinfection or not.

00:45:57.040 --> 00:46:00.480
Because we usually use you know

00:45:58.720 --> 00:46:01.680
antibodies as kind of a measure of that

00:46:00.480 --> 00:46:04.640
but

00:46:01.680 --> 00:46:06.000
it's not a perfect correlation. It's

00:46:04.640 --> 00:46:06.560
just one of the usually the easiest

00:46:06.000 --> 00:46:07.680
things

00:46:06.560 --> 00:46:10.240
to measure and kind of most

00:46:07.680 --> 00:46:11.520
straightforward things.

00:46:10.240 --> 00:46:13.440
So we don't know and that's what

00:46:11.520 --> 00:46:16.400
concerns myself and

00:46:13.440 --> 00:46:18.319
some other epidemiologists about

00:46:16.400 --> 00:46:19.040
like Sweden that is using basically herd

00:46:18.319 --> 00:46:21.680
immunity

00:46:19.040 --> 00:46:22.720
and protection of elderly

00:46:21.680 --> 00:46:25.839
and other vulnerable

00:46:22.720 --> 00:46:27.680
populations as their

00:46:25.839 --> 00:46:28.880
strategy. You know just let

00:46:27.680 --> 00:46:31.520
this come in,

00:46:28.880 --> 00:46:32.480
let's get a good population that has

00:46:31.520 --> 00:46:35.440
immunity to it

00:46:32.480 --> 00:46:36.640
and that's how we'll deal with it. But we

00:46:35.440 --> 00:46:39.200
don't know if that'll work.

00:46:36.640 --> 00:46:40.560
So we'll be looking at you know

00:46:39.200 --> 00:46:41.839
countries who are taking that strategy,

00:46:40.560 --> 00:46:43.359
we will be looking at people who have

00:46:41.839 --> 00:46:45.520
been known to be infected,

00:46:43.359 --> 00:46:47.359
and once we have good antibody tests we

00:46:45.520 --> 00:46:50.960
can kind of follow them

00:46:47.359 --> 00:46:52.640
over time to see if that immunity

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:55.680
wanes and if so how quickly

00:46:52.640 --> 00:46:57.200
or not. So the herd immunity

00:46:55.680 --> 00:47:00.319
aspect is something that also

00:46:57.200 --> 00:47:01.599
you know we know in theory but whether

00:47:00.319 --> 00:47:03.920
that will actually happen

00:47:01.599 --> 00:47:05.680
in practice it will be completely due to

00:47:03.920 --> 00:47:08.160
like the behavior of this

00:47:05.680 --> 00:47:09.520
virus and how it interacts with our

00:47:08.160 --> 00:47:12.240
own

00:47:09.520 --> 00:47:12.240
immune system.

00:47:12.800 --> 00:47:16.739
All right

00:47:18.160 --> 00:47:22.729
Let's see.

00:47:23.599 --> 00:47:28.079
All right from Rachel Graham: Do you

00:47:25.119 --> 00:47:31.119
believe this can be a chronic infection?

00:47:28.079 --> 00:47:34.480
We also don't know that.

00:47:31.119 --> 00:47:37.119
I think it's unlikely.

00:47:34.480 --> 00:47:38.480
Most of the human coronaviruses as

00:47:37.119 --> 00:47:40.880
far as we know do not

00:47:38.480 --> 00:47:41.680
cause a chronic infection. The common

00:47:40.880 --> 00:47:43.200
cold ones or

00:47:41.680 --> 00:47:45.359
you know SARS or MERS we haven't seen

00:47:43.200 --> 00:47:48.800
that at all.

00:47:45.359 --> 00:47:51.040
In the animal coronaviruses there is a

00:47:48.800 --> 00:47:52.079
feline one that can cause a chronic

00:47:51.040 --> 00:47:54.319
infection

00:47:52.079 --> 00:47:55.520
but that seems to kind of be an outlier

00:47:54.319 --> 00:47:56.160
and I talked about that a little bit in

00:47:55.520 --> 00:47:59.440
the article.

00:47:56.160 --> 00:47:59.440
So

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:04.880
I think it's unlikely but you know

00:48:02.800 --> 00:48:07.839
with biology with viruses

00:48:04.880 --> 00:48:08.880
until you have some evidence I would not

00:48:07.839 --> 00:48:13.119
say

00:48:08.880 --> 00:48:13.119
completely rule that out,

00:48:13.359 --> 00:48:17.520
anything is potential but I

00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:19.920
think that is not

00:48:17.520 --> 00:48:20.800
something that is going to be likely

00:48:19.920 --> 00:48:23.280
with this

00:48:20.800 --> 00:48:23.280
virus.

00:48:25.599 --> 00:48:37.280
Let's see.

00:48:34.319 --> 00:48:39.200
Sorry it's hard to answer and go on

00:48:37.280 --> 00:48:41.520
read these questions as well.

00:48:42.480 --> 00:48:47.440
So can you explain in simple terms

00:48:44.720 --> 00:48:51.200
why COVID-19 wasn't created in the lab?

00:48:47.440 --> 00:48:53.920
Maybe I can.

00:48:51.200 --> 00:48:54.400
I mean first I think it's unlikely

00:48:53.920 --> 00:48:56.160
that

00:48:54.400 --> 00:48:57.440
that happened. I know there are some

00:48:56.160 --> 00:49:01.040
really big advocates of that

00:48:57.440 --> 00:49:04.800
but I think what some people don't

00:49:01.040 --> 00:49:06.000
realize is how often these spillovers

00:49:04.800 --> 00:49:08.079
from

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:09.359
animals to people happen. Okay so there's

00:49:08.079 --> 00:49:12.640
even a term for it

00:49:09.359 --> 00:49:13.839
in the field we refer to it as

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:16.880
viral chatter.

00:49:13.839 --> 00:49:19.839
So these spillovers from

00:49:16.880 --> 00:49:20.319
animal populations to human populations

00:49:19.839 --> 00:49:22.880
of

00:49:20.319 --> 00:49:23.839
different types of viruses. And so we

00:49:22.880 --> 00:49:26.319
know this happens

00:49:23.839 --> 00:49:27.119
a lot and most of these spillovers

00:49:26.319 --> 00:49:29.599
are not

00:49:27.119 --> 00:49:31.200
documented so it often takes something

00:49:29.599 --> 00:49:34.240
that is pretty dramatic like

00:49:31.200 --> 00:49:37.280
right now like this coronavirus pandemic

00:49:34.240 --> 00:49:39.760
or like an Ebola outbreak so

00:49:37.280 --> 00:49:41.359
you know something where you have really

00:49:39.760 --> 00:49:44.960
clear symptoms

00:49:41.359 --> 00:49:45.280
you have a population where you can see

00:49:44.960 --> 00:49:48.720
this

00:49:45.280 --> 00:49:49.599
going through the people there

00:49:48.720 --> 00:49:52.640
and something that comes to the

00:49:49.599 --> 00:49:55.839
attention of public health authorities.

00:49:52.640 --> 00:49:57.760
So a lot of these

00:49:55.839 --> 00:49:59.520
spillovers are often dead ends and

00:49:57.760 --> 00:50:01.359
they're either they don't

00:49:59.520 --> 00:50:03.040
spread from human to human so they

00:50:01.359 --> 00:50:04.720
may go from animal to person but then

00:50:03.040 --> 00:50:08.559
not from person to person.

00:50:04.720 --> 00:50:10.319
So they die out or they can circulate

00:50:08.559 --> 00:50:12.160
maybe undetected for a long time. This is

00:50:10.319 --> 00:50:15.040
what happened with HIV.

00:50:12.160 --> 00:50:16.559
HIV actually is not a single spillover

00:50:15.040 --> 00:50:18.319
event. It didn't happen just once it

00:50:16.559 --> 00:50:19.119
happened multiple times with related

00:50:18.319 --> 00:50:20.480
viruses.

00:50:19.119 --> 00:50:21.599
That's why they're slightly different

00:50:20.480 --> 00:50:22.480
they came from slightly different

00:50:21.599 --> 00:50:24.800
animals

00:50:22.480 --> 00:50:27.040
from you know chimpanzees to sooty

00:50:24.800 --> 00:50:30.559
mangabeys to gorillas

00:50:27.040 --> 00:50:32.640
that independently went from

00:50:30.559 --> 00:50:34.079
those animal reservoirs into the human

00:50:32.640 --> 00:50:36.400
population and then

00:50:34.079 --> 00:50:38.480
spread from there but again we didn't

00:50:36.400 --> 00:50:38.960
detect them initially because they don't

00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:42.000
cause

00:50:38.960 --> 00:50:45.200
like you know symptoms as

00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:47.119
obvious maybe as Ebola.

00:50:45.200 --> 00:50:48.400
And they happened in populations that

00:50:47.119 --> 00:50:49.280
were you know not being carefully

00:50:48.400 --> 00:50:51.760
monitored,

00:50:49.280 --> 00:50:52.640
you know in many cases it was before

00:50:51.760 --> 00:50:55.119
we had a lot of

00:50:52.640 --> 00:50:57.119
the science to be able to detect them so

00:50:55.119 --> 00:51:00.559
you know that's why HIV and aids

00:50:57.119 --> 00:51:02.800
wasn't found until the 1980s in the

00:51:00.559 --> 00:51:05.200
United States when it hit populations

00:51:02.800 --> 00:51:06.400
that you could see okay something weird

00:51:05.200 --> 00:51:09.359
is happening here

00:51:06.400 --> 00:51:10.160
in these populations. But some of the

00:51:09.359 --> 00:51:12.319
earliest

00:51:10.160 --> 00:51:13.920
viruses crossed over into human

00:51:12.319 --> 00:51:15.119
populations you know almost a century

00:51:13.920 --> 00:51:18.880
before that

00:51:15.119 --> 00:51:21.119
and had been spreading undetected. So

00:51:18.880 --> 00:51:22.079
that's a long introduction into

00:51:21.119 --> 00:51:25.200
saying that I think it's

00:51:22.079 --> 00:51:26.640
it's just based on how everything works

00:51:25.200 --> 00:51:28.720
it is much more likely that this is a

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:31.680
natural spillover than

00:51:28.720 --> 00:51:33.200
something created in a lab. Looking at

00:51:31.680 --> 00:51:33.760
its genome sequence it doesn't have any

00:51:33.200 --> 00:51:37.440
markers

00:51:33.760 --> 00:51:40.640
of being you know altered by

00:51:37.440 --> 00:51:40.960
humans. I know and after that was kind

00:51:40.640 --> 00:51:43.839
of

00:51:40.960 --> 00:51:45.520
pointed out the other idea came that

00:51:43.839 --> 00:51:48.240
well okay but it was in the lab

00:51:45.520 --> 00:51:49.680
and then it had just gotten loose from

00:51:48.240 --> 00:51:52.720
there which

00:51:49.680 --> 00:51:54.480
is tougher to rule out but if so it

00:51:52.720 --> 00:51:56.400
was not a virus that ever was recorded

00:51:54.480 --> 00:51:57.920
as being in that laboratory, it was never

00:51:56.400 --> 00:52:02.400
identified or sequenced

00:51:57.920 --> 00:52:04.480
so and you know we,

00:52:02.400 --> 00:52:06.000
I can't say lab accidents never happen

00:52:04.480 --> 00:52:07.920
but they're certainly much less common

00:52:06.000 --> 00:52:09.599
than just those natural spillovers from

00:52:07.920 --> 00:52:11.359
wildlife reservoirs or other animal

00:52:09.599 --> 00:52:15.440
reservoirs into humans.

00:52:11.359 --> 00:52:17.280
So you know I think

00:52:15.440 --> 00:52:19.440
it's tough to prove a negative but I

00:52:17.280 --> 00:52:20.960
think it's much more likely that it

00:52:19.440 --> 00:52:24.000
was a natural event

00:52:20.960 --> 00:52:27.760
than some kind of a laboratory

00:52:24.000 --> 00:52:31.280
accident right. So maybe we'll do one

00:52:27.760 --> 00:52:33.839
final one here.

00:52:31.280 --> 00:52:33.839
Let's see.

00:52:38.400 --> 00:52:41.760
Is the idea that viruses

00:52:40.880 --> 00:52:45.520
evolve to become

00:52:41.760 --> 00:52:47.920
less pathogenic a rule of thumb?

00:52:45.520 --> 00:52:49.040
And that is one thing that that

00:52:47.920 --> 00:52:52.000
certainly people have

00:52:49.040 --> 00:52:53.359
put forward about this virus in

00:52:52.000 --> 00:52:56.400
particular and about viruses in

00:52:53.359 --> 00:52:58.640
general and it's not. I mean it's…

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:03.359
People see that we have identified

00:53:02.480 --> 00:53:06.559
multiple

00:53:03.359 --> 00:53:09.599
mutations in

00:53:06.559 --> 00:53:12.240
coronavirus lineages and I think people

00:53:09.599 --> 00:53:15.440
hear mutation and associate that

00:53:12.240 --> 00:53:17.599
with meaning as far as

00:53:15.440 --> 00:53:19.200
changing pathogenicity, either making it

00:53:17.599 --> 00:53:22.400
worse or making it

00:53:19.200 --> 00:53:23.520
you know less serious. That's not

00:53:22.400 --> 00:53:25.119
necessarily the case. You can have

00:53:23.520 --> 00:53:27.119
mutations that just do nothing. That's

00:53:25.119 --> 00:53:30.000
most of the mutations right.

00:53:27.119 --> 00:53:31.280
And there's this idea that if

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:33.599
something

00:53:31.280 --> 00:53:34.559
you know jumps from again maybe a

00:53:33.599 --> 00:53:37.599
non-human

00:53:34.559 --> 00:53:38.880
reservoir into people that over time

00:53:37.599 --> 00:53:42.000
if it co-evolves

00:53:38.880 --> 00:53:44.640
with humans that it will become

00:53:42.000 --> 00:53:46.640
you know mild or asymptomatic and

00:53:44.640 --> 00:53:48.559
that can happen sometimes but it's not

00:53:46.640 --> 00:53:50.640
something that necessarily happens. Again

00:53:48.559 --> 00:53:53.280
we had Ebola in west Africa

00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:54.720
that went through many many many

00:53:53.280 --> 00:53:57.760
chains of transmission

00:53:54.720 --> 00:53:58.559
from 2013 to 2016 before we were

00:53:57.760 --> 00:54:01.760
finally able to

00:53:58.559 --> 00:54:02.640
extinguish that outbreak. And there

00:54:01.760 --> 00:54:05.119
was really no

00:54:02.640 --> 00:54:08.000
evidence over that period of time that

00:54:05.119 --> 00:54:09.920
it was becoming less pathogenic.

00:54:08.000 --> 00:54:12.079
Mortality rates decreased in some cases

00:54:09.920 --> 00:54:13.440
but that seems to be more related to

00:54:12.079 --> 00:54:15.200
getting good care, just getting good

00:54:13.440 --> 00:54:17.200
supportive care and not to any actual

00:54:15.200 --> 00:54:20.559
properties of the virus itself.

00:54:17.200 --> 00:54:21.920
So with viruses and with

00:54:20.559 --> 00:54:24.319
you know evolution like that there's

00:54:21.920 --> 00:54:28.400
really no hard and fast rules,

00:54:24.319 --> 00:54:30.880
there's always lots of

00:54:28.400 --> 00:54:32.240
maybe general outcomes but even that

00:54:30.880 --> 00:54:33.839
doesn't always… you know there are always

00:54:32.240 --> 00:54:36.640
exceptions that prove the rules right.

00:54:33.839 --> 00:54:39.599
So I don't think we should assume

00:54:36.640 --> 00:54:41.839
that this is going to become

00:54:39.599 --> 00:54:43.920
less pathogenic or anything or evolve in

00:54:41.839 --> 00:54:45.839
some certain trajectory we just

00:54:43.920 --> 00:54:47.839
I think really don't know right now. And

00:54:45.839 --> 00:54:49.440
it will depend on

00:54:47.839 --> 00:54:51.200
a ton of different factors including

00:54:49.440 --> 00:54:53.200
whether it's you know maybe

00:54:51.200 --> 00:54:55.839
recombines with other coronaviruses

00:54:53.200 --> 00:54:56.640
or whether it sticks to itself or if

00:54:55.839 --> 00:54:59.839
it just

00:54:56.640 --> 00:55:02.559
hits on any particular mutations that

00:54:59.839 --> 00:55:03.920
would make it more fit and whether those

00:55:02.559 --> 00:55:06.799
mutations would make it

00:55:03.920 --> 00:55:07.520
more or less pathogenic, again I don't

00:55:06.799 --> 00:55:10.319
think we

00:55:07.520 --> 00:55:11.440
can necessarily foresee that right now.

00:55:10.319 --> 00:55:15.839
All right so with that

